**Correct answer: D — All of the above**

- **A. A physician responding to an emergency call** — Incorrect; a physician responding to an emergency call is exempt, but it is not the only exempt category, so A alone is incomplete.
- **B. A physician's driver when the physician responds to an emergency call** — Incorrect; the physician's driver responding to an emergency call is likewise exempt, but this is still only one of the listed categories.
- **C. The driver of a hospital ambulance on the way or from the place of accident or other emergency** — Incorrect; the ambulance driver going to or from the scene is exempt too, but again this is only one category.
- **D. All of the above** — Correct; all three fall among the categories exempt from the speed limit (Republic Act No. 4136, Sec. 35[c]).

**Republic Act No. 4136, Section 35(c) — Restriction as to speed.** The rates of speed hereinabove prescribed shall not apply to the following:

1. A physician or his driver when the former responds to emergency calls;
2. The driver of a hospital ambulance on the way to and from the place of accident or other emergency;
3. Any driver bringing a wounded or sick person for emergency treatment to a hospital, clinic, or any other similar place;
4. The driver of a motor vehicle belonging to the Armed Forces while in use for official purposes in times of riot, insurrection or invasion;
5. The driver of a vehicle, when he or his passengers are in pursuit of a criminal;
6. A law-enforcement officer who is trying to overtake a violator of traffic laws; and
7. The driver officially operating a motor vehicle of any fire department, provided that exemption shall not be construed to allow unless or unnecessary fast driving of drivers aforementioned.

**Correct answer: D — All of the above**

- **A. Criminal liability** — Incorrect; criminal liability attaches, but it is not the only liability, so this is incomplete.
- **B. Civil liability** — Incorrect; civil liability follows from the crime, but it is only one of the liabilities incurred.
- **C. Administrative liability** — Incorrect; administrative liability applies as well, so listing it alone is incomplete.
- **D. All of the above** — Correct; every person criminally liable is also civilly liable (Revised Penal Code, Art. 100), and abortion is an offense involving moral turpitude that grounds administrative penalties (Republic Act No. 2382, Sec. 24[8]), so all three attach.

**Revised Penal Code, Article 100 — Civil liability of a person guilty of felony.** Every person criminally liable for a felony is also civilly liable.

**Republic Act No. 2382** (amended by Republic Act No. 4224 and Republic Act No. 5946), **Section 24 — Grounds for reprimand, suspension or revocation of registration certificate.** Any of the following shall be sufficient ground for reprimanding a physician, or for suspending or revoking a certificate of registration as physician:

1. Conviction by a court of competent jurisdiction of any criminal offense involving moral turpitude;
2. Immoral or dishonorable conduct;
3. Insanity;
4. Fraud in the acquisition of the certificate of registration;
5. Gross negligence, ignorance or incompetence in the practice of his or her profession resulting in an injury to or death of the patient;
6. Addiction to alcoholic beverages or to any habit forming drug rendering him or her incompetent to practice his or her profession, or to any form of gambling;
7. False or extravagant or unethical advertisements wherein other things than his name, profession, limitation of practice, clinic hours, office and home address, are mentioned.
8. Performance of or aiding in any criminal abortion;
9. Knowingly issuing any false medical certificate;
10. Issuing any statement or spreading any news or rumor which is derogatory to the character and reputation of another physician without justifiable motive;
11. Aiding or acting as a dummy of an unqualified or unregistered person to practice medicine;
12. Violation of any provision of the Code of Ethics as approved by the Philippine Medical Association.

Every person criminally liable is also civilly liable. Criminal offenses involving moral turpitude is a ground for administrative penalties such as reprimand, or suspension or revocation of license to practice. Abortion, along with many other offenses done with malice, is an offense involving moral turpitude.

